+# Tool input validation
+
+| Field  | Value                |
+| ------ | -------------------- |
+| Issue  | re-cinq/HALEngine#37 |
+| Status | Draft                |
+
+The registry owns validation of model-supplied tool input against the same JSON Schema definition exposed to providers.
+
+## Contract
+
+1. Before executing a tool, the registry rejects missing required properties, incorrect declared types, and invalid enum members with a readable `ToolResponse.result` naming the property path and constraint, without invoking the executor or exposing input values in results or logs; accepted input, including unknown properties, reaches the executor exactly once without coercion or defaults, while unknown tool names still throw `Unknown tool: <name>`. ([validated by: rejects malformed tool input privately and passes accepted input through unchanged](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L14)).
+2. Static schemas compile at registration, with an uncompilable schema throwing an error naming the tool at that point, and the compiled schema is reused across ten executions without rereading the schema. ([validated by: compiles static schemas at registration and reuses them across ten executions](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L83)).
+3. Function-valued definitions resolve on each execution and validate against that call's schema, caching compilation by schema object identity rather than tool name. ([validated by: validates each dynamic definition and caches only by resolved schema identity](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L122)).
+
+## Delivery notes
+
+Validation is a tool result so the existing orchestrator can return it to the model within its five-round retry bound. The implementation uses a runtime JSON Schema validator dependency (Ajv unless the build prevents it), with no opt-out flag. The execute method's one-line comment points here for its contract.
+
+Implementation delivery also updates README.md, docs/adding-a-tool.md, and .specify/spec.md, adds a consumer-facing CHANGELOG.md entry, and makes a MINOR version bump. The migration note explains that executors no longer receive malformed input to hand-validate or to produce their own error messages. Output validation, authorization, timeouts, rate limits, and changes to unknown-tool behavior are separate work.
